<html>
<title>Switch Mnemonic</title>
<body>


<h1>Switch Mnemonic</h1>


<h2>Opcodes</h2>

<p>
There are a few CDP1802 opcodes with multiple mnemonics:
</p>
<ul>
<li><tt>33</tt>: <tt>BDF</tt>, <tt>BPZ</tt>, or <tt>BGE</tt></li>
<li><tt>38</tt>: <tt>NBR</tt> or <tt>SKP</tt></li>
<li><tt>3B</tt>: <tt>BNF</tt>, <tt>BM</tt>, or <tt>BL</tt></li>
<li><tt>76</tt>: <tt>SHRC</tt> or <tt>RSHR</tt></li>
<li><tt>7E</tt>: <tt>SHLC</tt> or <tt>RSHL</tt></li>
<li><tt>C8</tt>: <tt>NLBR</tt> or <tt>LSKP</tt></li>
</ul>

<p>
All variants are accepted in the assembler. The variant used will be stored as the memory type. This can also be changed via the procedure described in the <a href="DirectMarking.htm">Memory Type Selection</a> section, using the <tt>Switch</tt> marking type. When no type is specified, the first mnemonic listed for each opcode will be used.
</p>


<h2>Example</h2>

<p>
For example, if the type is set to <tt>Switch</tt> (1) and the <strong>Mark</strong> button (2) is pressed on address 0000 (hexadecimal) in the following example:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_switch_mark.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <tt>Switch</tt> type selection</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Mark</strong> button</li>
</ul>

<p>
the code will change to:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_switch_mark_result.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
Press <strong>Mark</strong> again on address 0000 (hexadecimal) and the code will change to:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_switch_mark_result2.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
</body>
</html>
